schema: concept-lineage
object:
  title: Quadratic Funding
  type: concept-lineage
  short_description: >-
    A matching-fund formula that rewards the *number* of contributors to a
    project rather than just the total dollar amount, so many small
    donations can out-earn one large donation; used by Gitcoin Grants rounds.
  source_traditions:
    - Mechanism design / public-goods funding theory (Buterin, Hitzig, Weyl)
    - Gitcoin Grants rounds (crypto-native implementation)
  toolkit_usage: >-
    Introduced here as the mechanism behind Gitcoin Grants' web3-native
    funding tier for gatherings, illustrated with "100 people giving $1 can
    out-earn one person giving $100."
  adjacent_meanings:
    - Traditional matching-gift programs (dollar-for-dollar, no breadth weighting)
    - Crowdfunding (raises the base amount but has no matching-pool mechanic)
  important_distinctions:
    - >-
      Quadratic funding (a matching-pool allocation formula, requires a
      sponsor-funded pool) vs. quadratic voting (a governance-decision
      mechanism using a personal credit budget) — related math, different
      use case; see the governance-mechanism-design article's treatment of
      quadratic voting.
  tensions:
    - >-
      Rewards breadth of donors, which can be gamed by Sybil attacks (many
      fake small donors); the article does not discuss Gitcoin's anti-Sybil
      safeguards.
  risks_of_flattening: >-
    Presenting "100 people giving $1 can out-earn one person giving $100" as
    a simple rule risks obscuring that the actual match depends on the
    specific round's formula, pool size, and anti-Sybil adjustments in
    effect at the time (the article itself flags that Gitcoin's rounds mix
    QF with other allocation mechanisms by domain).
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/funding-gatherings.md
    transformation: synthesized
    authorship: ai-assisted
